<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · South America</p><h1 class="st-title">Willoughbyland</h1><p class="st-meta">1650 CE – 1667 CE · capital Torarica</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">An English sugar colony on the Suriname river, founded from Barbados, that was lost to the Dutch in 1667 in exchange for New York.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1650 Francis, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-willoughby-5th-baron-willoughby-of-parham">Lord Willoughby of Parham</a>, the royalist governor of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/barbados">Barbados</a>, sent Anthony Rowse with settlers to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/suriname">Suriname</a> river in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guianas">Guiana</a>. In 1652 Willoughby visited the colony, and in 1662 he received a royal grant from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-ii-of-england">Charles II</a>. The capital was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torarica">Torarica</a>, a small town on the river.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Willoughbyland grew quickly to several thousand people, with some fifty <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> plantations worked by enslaved Africans. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sephardic-jews">Sephardic Jewish</a> settlers, some from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/dutch-brazil">Dutch Brazil</a>, were granted privileges and founded settlements along the river that later became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jodensavanne">Jodensavanne</a>. The writer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aphra-behn">Aphra Behn</a> claimed to have lived there and set her novel <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oroonoko">Oroonoko</a> in the colony. A governor ruled with an assembly of planters.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1667, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-anglo-dutch-war">Second Anglo-Dutch War</a>, a <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-zeeland">Zeeland</a> fleet under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abraham-crijnssen">Abraham Crijnssen</a> captured the colony. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-breda-1667">Treaty of Breda</a> that year confirmed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> possession of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/suriname">Suriname</a> in exchange for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> keeping <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/new-netherland">New Netherland</a>, renamed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>. Many English planters left by 1680.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/willoughbyland.jpg" alt="Willoughbyland" width="320" height="192"><figcaption>Image: Original: traditional Vector: Nicholas Shanks · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_England.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1650 CE – 1667 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Torarica</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Francis Willoughby</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Anglicanism, Judaism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>English</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Suriname</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>English Surinam, Surinam (English colony)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1658&amp;focus=willoughbyland" aria-label="Willoughbyland on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="3150 1959 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Willoughbyland lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M3470 2215L4970 663M3470 2215L4997 651M3470 2215L3471 2214"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4970 663h0M4997 651h0M3471 2214h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3470 2215h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3470 2215h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1658&amp;focus=willoughbyland">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
